{#
    Bill's `actionBarTemplate` for `commercial_document_view.html.twig` — the same role
    `_document_action_bar.html.twig` plays for PurchaseOrder, and for the same reason: `detail()` is
    the only GET route for this document, so there is nothing for a real tab bar to say here.

    What it carries beyond the flash messages `@Procurement/_nav.html.twig` used to render for every
    other purchase-side screen (this document has no app.js hook, so a flash has to be real, visible
    markup): the duplicate-invoice-number and received-elsewhere warnings, which are `report`-driven
    and belong at the very top of the page, above the summary cards, exactly where they sat before
    this screen joined the shared frame. `report` is passed in explicitly because the frame includes
    this template `with actionBarContext only` — nothing else on the page leaks into this slot.

    Parameters
    ----------
    report  required  the three-way-match report `VendorBillController::detail()` builds.
    bill    required  the vendor bill, for the duplicate-invoice-number notice's own wording.
#}
{% include '@Procurement/_purchase_flash_messages.html.twig' %}

{% if report.duplicateVendorInvoiceNumber %}
    <div class="flash flash-error">
        Vendor invoice {{ bill.vendorInvoiceNo }} is already on file for {{ bill.vendorName }}. Paying the same
        invoice twice is the most expensive clerical error in AP, and it happens because the same PDF arrives twice
        by email. Check before approving — this is a warning and not a block, because vendors do reuse their own
        numbers.
    </div>
{% endif %}

{# The fourth document in the three-way match: where the goods actually landed. A bill's warehouse
   is the only input to its frozen tax province (queue item 32), so a bill naming a building the
   receipts contradict was taxed at the wrong province's rates — and until this notice nothing in
   the estate said so. Warned and not blocked, exactly as the duplicate above is: a split delivery
   and a mis-booked receipt look identical from here, and only a human knows which this is. The
   sentence comes from the report so this screen and the timeline entry written at approval cannot
   disagree about a finding whose entire subject is two records disagreeing. #}
{% if report.receivedElsewhere %}
    <div class="flash flash-error" id="bill-received-elsewhere">
        {{ report.describeReceivedElsewhere }}
        Check the receipts before approving — a split delivery is legitimate, a receipt booked to
        the wrong warehouse is not, and they look the same from here.
    </div>
{% endif %}
